1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,340
All right, I'm ready for my next question.

2
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:10,070
In a world where rhinoceroses are domesticated pets,

3
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,080
who wins the Second World War?

4
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:16,480
Uganda.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:17,480
Defend.

6
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:20,950
Kenya rises to power on the export of rhinoceroses.

7
00:00:20,950 --> 00:00:23,150
A Central African power block is formed,

8
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:25,260
colonizing North Africa and Europe.

9
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:28,690
When war breaks out, no one can afford the luxury of a rhino.

10
00:00:28,690 --> 00:00:31,060
Kenya withers, Uganda triumphs.

11
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,200
Correct.

12
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:35,300
My turn.

13
00:00:37,170 --> 00:00:41,540
In a world where a piano is a weapon, not a musical instrument,

14
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:45,340
on what does Scott Joplin play "The Maple Leaf Rag"?

15
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:48,110
Tuned bayonets.

16
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:49,180
Defend.

17
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:50,110
Isn't it obvious?

18
00:00:50,710 --> 00:00:52,680
You're right. My apologies.

19
00:00:54,190 --> 00:00:57,720
What the hell are you guys playing?

20
00:00:57,720 --> 00:00:59,460
It's a game we invented.

21
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:01,660
It's called Counterfactuals.

22
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:04,030
We postulate an alternate world that differs from ours

23
00:01:04,030 --> 00:01:06,260
in one key aspect and then pose questions to each other.

24
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:08,200
It's fun for ages eight to 80.

25
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:09,630
Join us.

26
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:11,140
All right.

27
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:12,600
I like a good brainteaser.

28
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,200
Give it a whirl.

29
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:15,770
You're in luck, this is an easy one.

30
00:01:15,770 --> 00:01:21,350
In a world where mankind is ruled by a giant intelligent beaver,

31
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:23,810
what food is no longer consumed?

32
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:28,720
Uh... a BLT where the "B" stands for beaver?

33
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:29,950
I don't know.

34
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:32,020
Leonard, be serious.

35
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:33,790
We're playing a game here.

36
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:36,890
I can figure this out. Let's see.

37
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:39,660
Um, well, beavers eat tree bark.

38
00:01:39,660 --> 00:01:43,070
The only tree bark I know that humans consume is cinnamon.

39
00:01:43,070 --> 00:01:44,370
So, I'll say cinnamon.

40
00:01:44,370 --> 00:01:45,400
Incorrect.

41
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:48,910
Obviously, the answer is cheese Danish.

42
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:50,240
What?

43
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:52,980
In a world ruled by a giant beaver,

44
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:56,250
mankind builds many dams to please the beaver overlord.

45
00:01:56,250 --> 00:01:58,650
The low-lying city of Copenhagen is flooded.

46
00:01:58,650 --> 00:01:59,920
Thousands die.

47
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:03,990
Devastated, the Danes never invent their namesake pastry.

48
00:02:07,390 --> 00:02:09,190
How does one miss that?

49
00:02:09,490 --> 00:02:11,960
This is ridiculous.

50
00:02:11,960 --> 00:02:13,400
You're just making stuff up.

51
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:15,230
Is he always like this when he loses?

52
00:02:15,230 --> 00:02:16,630
Oh, yes.

53
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:20,470
You should've been here for the great giant tantrum of 2008.

54
00:02:22,110 --> 00:02:24,540
You bumped the table and you know it.

55
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:32,580
Perhaps it would be kinder to play a game more suited to his abilities.

56
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:36,390
We'll close our eyes and count to ten while you hide.

57
00:02:36,690 --> 00:02:38,860
I'm going to my room.

58
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:39,720
Very good, Leonard.

59
00:02:39,730 --> 00:02:42,090
But next time, don't tell us where you're hiding.

60
00:03:10,570 --> 00:03:13,970
Did you guys see the paper in The American Physics Journal on supersolids?

61
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:14,830
It's pretty interesting.

62
00:03:14,830 --> 00:03:16,410
This guy's working from a hypothesis where...

63
00:03:16,410 --> 00:03:18,410
Spoiler alert! Spoiler alert.

64
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:20,180
- What?  - Don't ruin it for me, man.

65
00:03:20,180 --> 00:03:23,010
I printed out a PDF to read on the potty.

66
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:25,120
On the potty? What are you, five?

67
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:27,120
It's a potty. What do you call it?

68
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,120
Toilet.

69
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:30,250
That's a little vulgar for the dinner table, don't you think?

70
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:32,360
Oh, and "potty" is okay?

71
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:34,490
"Potty" is innocent. "Potty" is adorable.

72
00:03:34,490 --> 00:03:37,260
What do you do in the potty, wee-wee?

73
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:39,600
If I don't have to boom-boom.

74
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:41,970
Greetings.

75
00:03:41,970 --> 00:03:44,140
You all remember Amy Farrah Fowler.

76
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:45,340
- Nice to see you.  - Hello.

77
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:46,870
Sorry we're late.

78
00:03:46,870 --> 00:03:48,440
I must take responsibility.

79
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:50,110
I had to stop for feminine hygiene supplies.

80
00:03:50,110 --> 00:03:51,280
- Aha.  - Okay.

81
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:53,440
I believe she's experiencing her menses.

82
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:55,480
Actually, I'm not.

83
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:57,450
In order to avoid surprises, I wear them all the time.

84
00:03:57,450 --> 00:03:58,650
- Aha.  - Okay.

85
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:02,020
"Toilet's" sounding pretty good now, huh?

86
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,460
Hey, look, it's Shamy.

87
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:07,490
Shamy?

88
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:10,660
A juvenile amalgamation of our names.

89
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:13,200
Sheldon, Amy-- Shamy.

90
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:14,530
Oh.

91
00:04:14,530 --> 00:04:16,570
I don't like that. Don't do that.

92
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:19,670
All righty. What's new?

93
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:22,470
Well, just recently, I learned that you refer to us as Shamy,

94
00:04:22,470 --> 00:04:23,470
and I don't like that.

95
00:04:23,470 --> 00:04:28,110
I got it. But what I was going for was, you know, how's your life?

96
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:29,910
Like everybody else's.

97
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:33,050
Subject to entropy, decay and eventual death.

98
00:04:34,420 --> 00:04:35,720
Thank you for asking.

99
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:37,520
Why is she not taking our order?

100
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:38,720
I should've warned you,

101
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:42,190
one has to navigate a labyrinth of social nonsense

102
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:43,860
before one can be fed here.

103
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:46,900
Really? I assumed an establishment called

104
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:49,700
the Cheesecake Factory would function more efficiently.

105
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:51,000
It's how they lure you in.

106
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,270
I believe it's called "bait and switch."

107
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:56,140
Okay, I'm just gonna walk away,

108
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,240
'cause... I don't want to be here.

109
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:04,180
So, this is nice.

110
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,020
First time we've all gotten together to eat.

111
00:05:06,020 --> 00:05:09,320
You're right, he's a festival of humdrum chitchat.

112
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,290
Okay, that's all I got.

113
00:05:12,290 --> 00:05:14,560
Howard, you're up.

114
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:18,060
Um... tell us about your work, Amy.

115
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:19,760
I doubt you'd understand.

116
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:22,500
Sheldon tells me you only have a master's degree.

117
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:26,900
Raj, do you have any questions for Amy?

118
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:33,310
I'm curious as to why we're not eating alone.

119
00:05:33,310 --> 00:05:35,380
They can't function without me.

120
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:40,220
I'm the social glue that holds this little group together.

121
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:41,690
You're welcome.

122
00:05:44,090 --> 00:05:46,960
Listen, can I talk to you about your girlfriend?

123
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:48,760
She's not my girlfriend.

124
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:50,690
She's a girl and she's my friend,

125
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:54,430
but there's no touching or unsanitary exchange of saliva.

126
00:05:56,230 --> 00:05:57,300
Got it.

127
00:05:57,300 --> 00:05:59,270
Although, for the record, on one occasion,

128
00:05:59,270 --> 00:06:01,910
she licked her thumb to remove raspberry jelly

129
00:06:01,910 --> 00:06:03,770
from the corner of my mouth.

130
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:06,180
It's an action we both regret to this day.

131
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:07,280
Uh-huh.

132
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:09,610
Anyway, I'm not sure she's the best fit

133
00:06:09,610 --> 00:06:13,250
for our little-- how should we call it-- rebel alliance.

134
00:06:13,250 --> 00:06:16,650
Oh, I never identified with the rebel alliance.

135
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:19,060
Despite their tendency to build Death Stars,

136
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:21,320
I've always been more of an empire man.

137
00:06:23,230 --> 00:06:24,260
Yeah, not my point.

138
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:25,500
I know what your point is.

139
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:27,530
You're intimidated by Amy's intellect.

140
00:06:27,530 --> 00:06:30,000
To that I say, buck up.

141
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,300
Okay, let me just get right to it.

142
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:35,640
Amy is judgmental, sanctimonious and frankly...

143
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:38,910
just obnoxious.

144
00:06:38,910 --> 00:06:40,680
So?

145
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:43,050
So we already have you for all that.

146
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:47,650
Are you suggesting I terminate my relationship with Amy?

147
00:06:47,650 --> 00:06:49,050
No, no, of course not.

148
00:06:49,050 --> 00:06:52,160
Just have your relationship someplace else.

149
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:55,760
May I point out that for eight long months,

150
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,630
I suffered in silence as your female companion

151
00:06:58,630 --> 00:07:03,700
filled our apartment with her off-key country music caterwauling,

152
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:07,380
the unappetizing spectacle of her grinding a pumice stone

153
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:10,440
against her calloused feet in our living room,

154
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:12,420
and night after night of uninformative

155
00:07:12,420 --> 00:07:16,380
TV documentaries about the Jersey Shore.

156
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:20,680
Suffered in silence?

157
00:07:20,690 --> 00:07:22,690
Yes.

158
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:26,960
And I'd thank you to do the same.

159
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:30,890
Reall-- Silence?!

160
00:07:34,670 --> 00:07:36,770
Nothing makes beer taste better

161
00:07:36,770 --> 00:07:40,870
than cool clear Rocky Mountain spring water.

162
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:43,570
Where are the Rocky Mountains, anyway?

163
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:47,440
Philadelphia.

164
00:07:47,450 --> 00:07:48,480
Really?

165
00:07:48,480 --> 00:07:49,910
I thought they were out West someplace.

166
00:07:49,910 --> 00:07:51,050
Think about it, Raj.

167
00:07:51,050 --> 00:07:54,420
Where did the movie Rocky take place?

168
00:07:54,420 --> 00:07:56,990
Philadelphia.

169
00:07:56,990 --> 00:07:58,960
Okay, now I get it.

170
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:01,320
So this is the plan?

171
00:08:01,330 --> 00:08:02,780
From now on,

172
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:05,460
we're just gonna hide out in here to avoid the Shamy?

173
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:07,060
I'm very comfortable here.

174
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:11,600
Penny, dear, why don't you shoot another silver bullet my way?

175
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:13,640
Get one yourself.

176
00:08:13,640 --> 00:08:16,910
Ooh, somebody's been taking bitchy pills.

177
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:20,540
God, he's an ass when he drinks.

178
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:21,910
Oh, he's an ass when he doesn't.

179
00:08:21,910 --> 00:08:24,110
You just don't hear it.

180
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:27,050
Think we need to start entertaining the possibility

181
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:28,920
that the Shamy could go on for years.

182
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:31,620
Well, if that's the case, Penny will have to get satellite TV

183
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:34,320
and maybe once a week run a vacuum through this place.

184
00:08:38,430 --> 00:08:40,360
I thought you were going to talk to Sheldon.

185
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:42,230
- I did.  - Well, what'd he say?

186
00:08:42,230 --> 00:08:44,370
Well, he pointed out that he kinda sorta had to...

187
00:08:44,370 --> 00:08:45,840
put up with you.

188
00:08:45,840 --> 00:08:47,800
Kinda sorta had to?

189
00:08:47,810 --> 00:08:49,110
I didn't agree with him.

190
00:08:49,110 --> 00:08:50,110
Well, you defended me, right?

191
00:08:50,110 --> 00:08:52,940
I tried, but...

192
00:08:55,050 --> 00:08:59,250
...he made a fairly well-reasoned argument.

193
00:08:59,250 --> 00:09:02,520
You're not doing that right.

194
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:03,520
What?

195
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:04,750
Gimme.

196
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:05,990
- No.  - Trust me.

197
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:08,690
- No!  - I do this for my mom all the time.

198
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:11,860
See?

199
00:09:11,860 --> 00:09:14,000
With the grain.

200
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,600
Wow, that is better.

201
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:19,040
And someday, when you have varicose veins,

202
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:20,940
I'll show you how to massage them.

203
00:09:24,610 --> 00:09:27,880
Oh, God, never again.

204
00:09:27,880 --> 00:09:30,850
I assume by "never again," you mean never again will you drink

205
00:09:30,850 --> 00:09:33,250
all of Penny's beer, then run down to the gas station

206
00:09:33,250 --> 00:09:35,450
for a couple of 40s, a box of Slim Jims

207
00:09:35,450 --> 00:09:39,390
and the latest issue of Bombay Badonkadonks.

208
00:09:40,260 --> 00:09:42,560
I was homesick.

209
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:44,660
The highlight of the evening was when you showed us

210
00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:46,760
your Bollywood break dancing skills.

211
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:56,510
That's very offensive.

212
00:09:56,510 --> 00:09:58,610
Yeah, we all thought so.

213
00:09:58,610 --> 00:10:00,910
Oh, no.

214
00:10:00,910 --> 00:10:01,910
What?

215
00:10:01,910 --> 00:10:04,280
It's John and Yoko.

216
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:07,020
More like Yoko and Yoko.

217
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:08,720
Greetings. Hey.

218
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:09,950
I brought Amy here

219
00:10:09,950 --> 00:10:12,190
to show her some of the work I'm doing.

220
00:10:12,190 --> 00:10:14,590
It's very impressive, for theoretical work.

221
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:18,730
Do I detect a hint of condescension?

222
00:10:18,730 --> 00:10:20,930
I'm sorry, was I being too subtle?

223
00:10:22,200 --> 00:10:24,370
I meant compared to the real-world applications

224
00:10:24,370 --> 00:10:26,400
of neurobiology, theoretical physics is--

225
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:28,600
what's the word I'm looking for?-- hmm, cute.

226
00:10:30,540 --> 00:10:34,310
Are you suggesting the work of a neurobiologist

227
00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:37,680
like Babinski could ever rise to the significance

228
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:41,020
of a physicist like Clark Maxwell or Dirac?

229
00:10:41,020 --> 00:10:42,720
I'm stating it outright.

230
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:44,720
Babinski eats Dirac for breakfast

231
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:47,620
and defecates Clarke-Maxwell.

232
00:10:49,460 --> 00:10:51,630
You take that back.

233
00:10:53,460 --> 00:10:54,500
Absolutely not.

234
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:55,730
My colleagues and I are mapping

235
00:10:55,730 --> 00:10:57,700
the neurological substrates that subserve

236
00:10:57,700 --> 00:10:58,870
global information processing,

237
00:10:58,870 --> 00:11:00,970
which is required for all cognitive reasoning,

238
00:11:00,970 --> 00:11:04,670
including scientific inquiry, making my research ipso facto

239
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:06,010
prior in the ordo cognoscendi.

240
00:11:06,010 --> 00:11:08,710
That means it's better than his research,

241
00:11:08,710 --> 00:11:10,710
and by extension, of course, yours.

242
00:11:13,180 --> 00:11:15,520
I'm sorry, I'm-I'm still trying

243
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:19,820
to work on the defecating Clark Maxwell, so...

244
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:24,360
Excuse me, but a grand unified theory,

245
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:26,930
insofar as it explains everything,

246
00:11:26,930 --> 00:11:30,000
will ipso facto explain neurobiology.

247
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:31,600
Yes, but if I'm successful,

248
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:34,100
I will be able to map and reproduce your thought processes

249
00:11:34,100 --> 00:11:36,010
in deriving a grand unified theory,

250
00:11:36,010 --> 00:11:38,870
and therefore, subsume your conclusions under my paradigm.

251
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:42,410
That's the rankest psychologism,

252
00:11:42,410 --> 00:11:44,810
and was conclusively revealed as hogwash

253
00:11:44,820 --> 00:11:46,950
by Gottlob Frege in the 1890s!

254
00:11:46,950 --> 00:11:50,050
We appear to have reached an impasse.

255
00:11:50,050 --> 00:11:51,720
I agree.

256
00:11:51,720 --> 00:11:55,360
I move our relationship terminate immediately.

257
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,360
Seconded.

258
00:11:57,360 --> 00:11:58,460
There being no objections...

259
00:12:01,200 --> 00:12:02,900
...the motion carries.

260
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:04,870
Good day, Amy Farrah Fowler.

261
00:12:04,870 --> 00:12:06,870
Good day, Sheldon Cooper.

262
00:12:11,340 --> 00:12:12,880
Women, huh?

263
00:12:12,880 --> 00:12:14,410
Can't live with them,

264
00:12:14,410 --> 00:12:17,810
can't successfully refute their hypotheses.

265
00:12:17,810 --> 00:12:19,980
Amen to that.

266
00:12:26,380 --> 00:12:27,750
Hey, Sheldon.

267
00:12:27,750 --> 00:12:29,390
Hello.

268
00:12:30,690 --> 00:12:33,320
I hear you broke up with Amy.

269
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:37,760
A breakup would imply she was my girlfriend.

270
00:12:37,760 --> 00:12:39,360
She was a girl who was my friend

271
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:42,800
who is now a girl who is not my friend.

272
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:46,400
Wow. That's like the worst country song ever.

273
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:50,670
So, how are you doing?

274
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:51,940
Regarding what?

275
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:53,080
Amy.

276
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:55,180
I don't follow.

277
00:12:55,180 --> 00:13:00,420
Well, breakups-- or whatever the hell this is-- can be tough.

278
00:13:00,420 --> 00:13:02,420
Penny, I assure you, I'm fine.

279
00:13:02,420 --> 00:13:05,590
My relationship with Amy was purely intellectual,

280
00:13:05,590 --> 00:13:08,030
There were no emotional bonds,

281
00:13:08,030 --> 00:13:10,360
the severing of which would cause me any discomfort.

282
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:13,030
The relationship simply outlived its utility,

283
00:13:13,030 --> 00:13:16,000
and I'm continuing on with my life as before.

284
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,900
Okay. Good.

285
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:21,740
Now, if you'll excuse me, I'm off to buy a pussycat.

286
00:13:23,980 --> 00:13:25,610
I gotta tell you guys,

287
00:13:25,610 --> 00:13:27,180
I'm a little worried about Sheldon.

288
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:30,010
We're all a little worried about Sheldon.

289
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:33,050
No, I mean since the Shamy hit a reef.

290
00:13:33,050 --> 00:13:35,320
Oh, I thought you were just making a generalization:

291
00:13:35,320 --> 00:13:36,750
"You know, I'm worried about Sheldon

292
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:38,960
"someday setting off a low-yield nuclear device

293
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:41,690
because the cafeteria ran out of lime Jell-O."

294
00:13:42,490 --> 00:13:44,090
What does "hit a reef" mean?

295
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:45,630
Uh, went splitsville.

296
00:13:45,630 --> 00:13:46,630
Pardon?

297
00:13:46,630 --> 00:13:49,200
Turned to boom-boom.

298
00:13:50,970 --> 00:13:53,440
I think Sheldon really misses Amy.

299
00:13:53,440 --> 00:13:57,710
You should lend him your copy of Bombay Badonkadonks.

300
00:13:57,710 --> 00:13:59,880
He got a cat to keep him company.

301
00:13:59,880 --> 00:14:00,880
You're kidding.

302
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:02,150
He takes it everywhere:

303
00:14:02,150 --> 00:14:03,980
to bed, to the bathroom.

304
00:14:03,980 --> 00:14:05,950
He takes the kitty to the potty?

305
00:14:05,950 --> 00:14:08,550
I thought we discussed the P-word.

306
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:10,120
Don't try to change me, dude.

307
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:12,120
I am what I am.

308
00:14:12,120 --> 00:14:15,430
Oh, gentlemen.

309
00:14:15,430 --> 00:14:17,490
Hey. Aren't you going to introduce us

310
00:14:17,500 --> 00:14:19,160
to your little friend?

311
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:20,630
My apologies.

312
00:14:20,630 --> 00:14:25,400
Raj, Howard, I'd like you to meet Dr. Robert Oppenheimer.

313
00:14:26,370 --> 00:14:28,670
Hello. Hi.

314
00:14:28,670 --> 00:14:29,940
Now, if you'll excuse me,

315
00:14:29,940 --> 00:14:33,480
the father of the atomic bomb wants a saucer of milk.

316
00:14:35,310 --> 00:14:37,580
Okay, I get it. We're worried about Sheldon.

317
00:14:37,580 --> 00:14:39,050
Yeah.

318
00:14:41,750 --> 00:14:43,490
Hey.

319
00:14:43,490 --> 00:14:46,020
Oh, no.

320
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:48,630
Robert Oppenheimer was lonely.

321
00:14:48,630 --> 00:14:52,160
So you decided to get the whole Manhattan Project?

322
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:56,200
Yes. This is Enrico Fermi, Richard Feynman,

323
00:14:56,200 --> 00:14:56,970
Edward Teller,

324
00:14:56,970 --> 00:14:58,300
Otto Frisch

325
00:14:58,300 --> 00:15:01,170
and Zazzles.

326
00:15:01,170 --> 00:15:02,270
Zazzles?

327
00:15:02,270 --> 00:15:04,510
I was going to name him Hermann von Helmholtz,

328
00:15:04,510 --> 00:15:07,640
but he's so zazzy.

329
00:15:09,980 --> 00:15:12,820
Okay, we need to talk.

330
00:15:12,820 --> 00:15:14,050
About what?

331
00:15:14,050 --> 00:15:15,690
Cats, Sheldon.

332
00:15:15,690 --> 00:15:17,690
You're clearly upset about Amy being gone,

333
00:15:17,690 --> 00:15:19,890
and you're trying to replace her with a bunch of cats.

334
00:15:19,890 --> 00:15:21,490
- Clowder. - What?

335
00:15:21,490 --> 00:15:24,860
A group of cats is a clowder. Or a glaring.

336
00:15:24,860 --> 00:15:25,900
Okay, yeah, fine.

337
00:15:25,900 --> 00:15:27,600
It's the kind of thing you ought to know

338
00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:29,270
now that we have one.

339
00:15:29,270 --> 00:15:32,540
Terrific. My point is, you need to face up

340
00:15:32,540 --> 00:15:34,510
to what you're feeling with this breakup.

341
00:15:34,510 --> 00:15:35,740
It wasn't a breakup.

342
00:15:35,740 --> 00:15:37,640
A breakup would imply that Amy was my girlfriend.

343
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:40,180
Okay, I got it, I got it; she's not your girlfriend.

344
00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:41,550
Now listen to me.

345
00:15:41,550 --> 00:15:45,120
I know about loneliness.

346
00:15:45,120 --> 00:15:47,450
I know about trying to replace someone with other stuff.

347
00:15:47,450 --> 00:15:50,350
When I broke up with Penny, I got back into my cello,

348
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:52,460
I built a bunch of model rockets,

349
00:15:52,460 --> 00:15:56,990
I got those weightlifting gloves and that five-pound dumbbell.

350
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,360
You didn't break up, she dumped you.

351
00:16:00,370 --> 00:16:02,300
She didn't dump me.

352
00:16:02,300 --> 00:16:03,700
It was mutual!

353
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:05,370
I was there. She dumped you.

354
00:16:05,370 --> 00:16:06,740
Okay, fine.

355
00:16:06,740 --> 00:16:08,040
Live with cats.

356
00:16:08,040 --> 00:16:09,310
Be like my Aunt Nancy.

357
00:16:09,310 --> 00:16:10,670
She had dozens of them.

358
00:16:10,680 --> 00:16:13,140
And do you know what happened after she died? They ate her.

359
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:15,810
You don't have to sell me on cats, Leonard.

360
00:16:15,810 --> 00:16:17,050
I'm already a fan.

361
00:16:21,450 --> 00:16:25,720
All right, fellas, who's in the mood for Fancy Feast?

362
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:28,990
Well, that's not fancy at all.

363
00:16:33,470 --> 00:16:35,470
Hi, Mrs. Cooper. Thanks for coming.

364
00:16:35,470 --> 00:16:36,530
Where is he?

365
00:16:36,530 --> 00:16:37,700
He's in his bedroom.

366
00:16:37,700 --> 00:16:39,900
Now, when you said on the phone

367
00:16:39,910 --> 00:16:42,410
he broke up with a girl, you meant an actual girl,

368
00:16:42,410 --> 00:16:44,910
not something you kids whipped up in a lab?

369
00:16:44,910 --> 00:16:46,440
No, she's real.

370
00:16:46,450 --> 00:16:48,480
Did they sin?

371
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:50,510
No, no, it's not like that.

372
00:16:50,520 --> 00:16:52,620
It's, uh...

373
00:16:52,620 --> 00:16:54,320
I don't know what it's like.

374
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:56,250
But there is something I should prepare you for.

375
00:16:56,250 --> 00:16:59,920
Oh, relax, Leonard, I have raised that boy.

376
00:16:59,930 --> 00:17:02,460
I've seen him at his best, I've seen him at his worst.

377
00:17:02,460 --> 00:17:05,100
There's nothing he can do that'll surprise me.

378
00:17:05,100 --> 00:17:06,930
Hold on to that thought.

379
00:17:08,300 --> 00:17:09,730
Come in.

380
00:17:13,510 --> 00:17:15,710
Surprise.

381
00:17:15,710 --> 00:17:18,510
Mom, what an unexpected pleasure.

382
00:17:18,510 --> 00:17:22,350
My, my, that's a powerful smell.

383
00:17:22,350 --> 00:17:26,350
I'd like you to meet Oppenheimer, Frisch, Panofsky,

384
00:17:26,350 --> 00:17:28,090
Feynman, Weisskopf...

385
00:17:28,090 --> 00:17:29,250
Yeah, I get it.

386
00:17:29,250 --> 00:17:31,020
You got a lot of cats

387
00:17:31,020 --> 00:17:33,060
and you gave 'em cute Jewish names.

388
00:17:33,060 --> 00:17:34,930
What are you doing here?

389
00:17:34,930 --> 00:17:36,160
Leonard called.

390
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:39,400
And he said that you were pining for a young lady.

391
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:41,200
Oh, that's preposterous.

392
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:43,230
I'm not pining over anyone.

393
00:17:43,240 --> 00:17:46,340
Oh, lambchop, we can quibble what to call it,

394
00:17:46,340 --> 00:17:49,910
but I think we can both agree it's creepy.

395
00:17:49,910 --> 00:17:52,010
I do not agree.

396
00:17:52,010 --> 00:17:54,380
Cats make wonderful companions.

397
00:17:54,380 --> 00:17:58,650
They don't argue or question my intellectual authority,

398
00:17:58,650 --> 00:18:00,520
and this little guy here,

399
00:18:00,520 --> 00:18:03,790
I think you'll find to be quite zazzy.

400
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:08,560
You should have called sooner.

401
00:18:09,660 --> 00:18:11,900
Shelly! Dinner's ready!

402
00:18:11,900 --> 00:18:12,930
Coming!

403
00:18:12,930 --> 00:18:14,200
No cats!

404
00:18:19,670 --> 00:18:21,170
What is she doing here?

405
00:18:21,170 --> 00:18:22,470
I called her.

406
00:18:22,470 --> 00:18:23,510
Your mother thinks

407
00:18:23,510 --> 00:18:25,480
you might be losing your mind over me.

408
00:18:25,480 --> 00:18:28,110
As a neurobiologist, I was curious.

409
00:18:28,110 --> 00:18:31,820
Well, rest assured, I am in full possession of my faculties.

410
00:18:31,820 --> 00:18:34,920
25 cats!

411
00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:38,220
Oh, God bless you, dear.

412
00:18:38,220 --> 00:18:40,590
Sheldon, sit down. Let's talk.

413
00:18:40,590 --> 00:18:43,390
All right, but you're not fooling me.

414
00:18:43,400 --> 00:18:44,630
Whenever you say we have to talk,

415
00:18:44,630 --> 00:18:45,860
it means you want me to listen.

416
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:46,860
Then stop talking.

417
00:18:46,870 --> 00:18:48,670
Yes, ma'am.

418
00:18:48,670 --> 00:18:50,770
Now, the reason I called Amy over was

419
00:18:50,770 --> 00:18:53,340
to find out what type of person she is.

420
00:18:53,340 --> 00:18:55,010
And after chatting a bit,

421
00:18:55,010 --> 00:18:57,340
I have to say that while she is a

422
00:18:57,340 --> 00:19:00,850
perfectly unique young woman,

423
00:19:00,850 --> 00:19:04,350
you two are definitely not suited for each other.

424
00:19:04,350 --> 00:19:07,150
That's a peculiar conclusion.

425
00:19:07,150 --> 00:19:09,620
By any standard, Amy is more similar to me

426
00:19:09,620 --> 00:19:10,920
than anyone I've ever met.

427
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:13,720
Oh, I'm sorry, Shelly, I can't see it.

428
00:19:13,730 --> 00:19:16,630
Well, whether you see it or not is irrelevant.

429
00:19:16,630 --> 00:19:18,630
I can't see subatomic particles,

430
00:19:18,630 --> 00:19:20,500
but nevertheless, they're there.

431
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:21,600
Excellent point.

432
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:23,330
A physics point.

433
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:25,870
Touche

434
00:19:25,870 --> 00:19:28,210
Well, putting aside the pig Latin,

435
00:19:28,210 --> 00:19:30,040
it's a good thing that

436
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:32,010
you two decided to end the relationship

437
00:19:32,010 --> 00:19:34,450
so I didn't have to end it for you.

438
00:19:36,950 --> 00:19:38,480
Amy, after consideration,

439
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:40,680
I believe we may have acted rashly.

440
00:19:40,690 --> 00:19:42,550
I propose we resume our relationship

441
00:19:42,550 --> 00:19:45,060
and attempt to resolve our differences.

442
00:19:45,060 --> 00:19:46,990
I'll agree to that only if you'll stipulate

443
00:19:46,990 --> 00:19:49,090
that 80% of our difficulties were caused by you.

444
00:19:49,090 --> 00:19:50,900
- I'll go as high as 40. - Sixty-five.

445
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:52,430
Done.

446
00:19:52,430 --> 00:19:54,430
You understand that moving forward,

447
00:19:54,430 --> 00:19:57,900
we deal with the fact that my mother does not approve of you?

448
00:19:57,900 --> 00:20:00,870
I do. I find being cast

449
00:20:00,870 --> 00:20:03,570
in the role of bad girl oddly titillating.

450
00:20:05,210 --> 00:20:06,980
Would you like to see my cats?

451
00:20:06,980 --> 00:20:09,110
I would. I love cats.

452
00:20:09,110 --> 00:20:10,450
They're the epitome of indifference.

453
00:20:10,450 --> 00:20:14,250
Ah, then you may find Zazzles a little cloying.

454
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:17,120
I saw what you did there.

455
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:19,190
He thinks he's such a smarty pants.

456
00:20:19,190 --> 00:20:20,590
He's no different from any man.

457
00:20:20,590 --> 00:20:22,510
You tell 'em not to do something,

458
00:20:22,510 --> 00:20:23,930
certainly that's all  they want to do.

459
00:20:23,930 --> 00:20:25,430
If I hadn't told my brother Stumpy

460
00:20:25,430 --> 00:20:27,400
not to clear out the wood chipper by hand,

461
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:28,970
we'd still be calling him Edward.

462
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:31,740
Now, don't you move.

463
00:20:31,740 --> 00:20:33,340
I'll bring over all the food.

464
00:20:33,340 --> 00:20:35,440
No, no, no, I can do it.

465
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:39,440
Well, isn't that sweet?

466
00:20:46,550 --> 00:20:48,180
Thank you, Amy.

467
00:20:48,190 --> 00:20:49,690
Here's your cat.

468
00:20:50,790 --> 00:20:52,490
And here's your $20.

469
00:20:53,420 --> 00:20:55,120
Next!

470
00:20:55,130 --> 00:20:57,060
Thank you, Amy.

471
00:20:57,060 --> 00:20:58,260
Here's your cat.

472
00:20:59,130 --> 00:21:01,100
And here's your $20.

473
00:21:02,530 --> 00:21:04,630
Next.

